# Fix Upstream, Don't Fork

> **Scope:** Any code that depends on a library we own — arango-sparql-py,
> arango-query-core, r2g / r2g-arango, arango-schema-analyzer, arango-cypher-py,
> arango-entity-resolution, or any other library the team maintains. Every one of
> these is also a consumer of the others, so this applies inside the libraries too.

> **"We own the library. A gap in it is an issue there, not a fork here."**

Written after a 2026-10-15 finding in contextual-data-fabric: its English→SPARQL
translator was never the library's. In July, arango-sparql-py could only check a
generated query against one schema, so the consumer took the library's model client
and wrote its own prompt, check and repair loop around it. That was a reasonable
call — but no library issue was filed and nothing in the code said it was
temporary. When the shared engine gained the seam the consumer needed, nobody
noticed. Three months later there were three implementations, the design record
still said the engine was reused, and the library's docstring named a function the
consumer never imported.

The same audit found the pattern again around arango-entity-resolution: a consumer
guard that copies the resolver's checks line for line, configuration defects worked
around in a comment instead of filed, and a service that declares the library as a
dependency, says in its docstring that it runs the library's pipeline, and imports
none of it.

## When an owned library falls short

| Do, in this order | Not |
| --- | --- |
| 1. **Check it isn't already there** — `main`, open branches, and the other org's copy | Assume the version you have pinned is the latest |
| 2. **Fix it in the library** — we own it; open the pull request there | Patch around it here because this is the repo you have open |
| 3. **Or file it there** — a bug or feature request on the repo that leads, saying what the consumer needs and why | File it on the mirror, or mention it only in this repo's pull request |
| 4. **Only if this repo cannot wait** for a library release and a pin bump — a temporary workaround, held to the rules below | A permanent workaround nobody can find |

**Which repo leads.** Most of these libraries exist under two owners, and which one
leads has changed during 2026. Do not trust the remotes of your clone — a fresh
clone has only the URL you were handed. The repository with recent pull requests is
the one that leads:

```sh
gh pr list --repo <owner>/<name> --state all --limit 5
```

Run it against both candidates. If neither has recent pull requests, ask.

## A workaround that is allowed

- **Behind the seam the library would provide**, so removing it touches one place:
  an adapter, or a wrapper with the library's own shape.
- **Marked where it lives**, on one line at the code that works around the library:

  ```python
  # upstream: <org>/arango-sparql-py#<n> — remove when the engine accepts a caller's validator
  ```

  `tests/test_upstream_markers.py` holds the format: an `owner/repo#number` issue a
  person can open, and a `remove when` condition someone can check. (Its source is
  `templates/tests/test_upstream_markers.py` in arango-shared-memory.)
- **Listed in this repo's known-debt issue**, if it keeps one, with the same link.
- **Removed in the pin bump that ships the fix**, not in a later cleanup: when you
  bump a pin, search for `upstream:` markers that name that library.

## What counts as a fork

| Fork | Why it costs |
| --- | --- |
| Taking a library's low-level piece (a client, a parser) and writing your own version of its main entry point around it | Fixes and new capabilities land in the library's version and never reach yours |
| Copying library code into the consumer | The two copies drift |
| Patching the library at runtime | The next pin bump breaks it without a word |
| Reaching past the library's public API into its internals | Internals move without a release note |
| Defining a public function or class with the same name and job as one the library exports | Readers take one for the other |
| Declaring a library as a dependency and reimplementing what you would have called | The dependency says one thing and the code does another |
| Holding a pin back to dodge a bug, with no issue | Nobody knows when it can move |

A fork that is deliberate and permanent — the consumer needs something the library
should not hold — is a **design record (ADR) in the consumer** plus an **issue in the
library** for the seam that would let the two share the rest. It is never a silent
decision.

## The other side: claims about the other repo

A library that says a consumer uses it ("this is what the fabric calls") must be able
to point at the consumer's import. A consumer that says it uses a library ("runs the
entity-resolution pipeline") must import it. If either cannot, the claim is a plan,
and it is written as one.

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Consequence |
| --- | --- |
| "The library can't do X, so I'll write X here" | A fork nobody tracks |
| A workaround with no `upstream:` marker | Nobody removes it when the fix ships |
| An issue filed in the consumer instead of the library | The library's maintainers never see it |
| A library defect documented only in a config comment | The next consumer hits it too |
| "It's our library, I'll fix it there later" | Later is after the third implementation |

**We own the library: fix it there, or say where it will be fixed.**
